# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Armat — local environment
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the two values from your Supabase
# project (Project Settings → API). `.env.local` is git-ignored.
#
# Supabase is used for ONE purpose: storing newsletter email addresses. All
# educational content lives in local TypeScript files under src/data.
#
# Both variables are NEXT_PUBLIC_ on purpose — the anon key is meant to be
# public and is protected by row level security, not by secrecy.
#
# NEVER put the service_role key in this file or in any NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable.
# It bypasses row level security and would be shipped to every visitor.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Contact form — SMTP
#
# Used ONLY by the server route /api/contact. None of these carry the
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, so they never reach the browser bundle — that is the
# whole reason the contact form posts to a server route instead of talking to
# a mail service directly. Do not add the prefix to any of them.
#
# SMTP_SECURE=true for implicit TLS on port 465; leave it false for 587, which
# upgrades via STARTTLS. If your provider gave you a dedicated app password,
# use that as SMTP_PASSWORD rather than the account's own password.
#
# CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL must be an address the server is allowed to send as — it
# defaults to SMTP_USER. The reader's own address is set as Reply-To, never as
# the sender, because that would fail SPF and land the message in spam.
# CONTACT_TO_EMAIL is where messages arrive; it defaults to the site's
# published contact address.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_SECURE=false
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL=
CONTACT_TO_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visit map — basemap tiles (Stadia Maps)
#
# The values below are Armat's production basemap: Stadia Maps' `alidade_smooth`
# raster style. Copy them as-is. They are configuration, not credentials —
# there is nothing to fill in and nothing to keep secret.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_URL         Leaflet XYZ template. Must contain {z}, {x}
#                                  and {y}. `{r}` is Leaflet's retina
#                                  placeholder: it becomes "@2x" on HiDPI
#                                  screens and "" everywhere else.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION Copyright line rendered on the map. Copy it
#                                  exactly — it is the credit the tile licence
#                                  requires, not a caption to reword.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_MAX_ZOOM    Highest zoom the style serves. Stadia
#                                  documents 20 for this style.
#
# ## Authentication: none in the browser, on purpose
#
# Stadia authenticates production traffic by *registered domain*, validating the
# Origin/Referer headers the browser already sends. So there is no API key here,
# no token in the URL, and no authentication code in the app. Instead, someone
# with dashboard access must authorize the site's real hostname(s) — see the
# checklist in PROJECT_STATE.md §46.
#
# Never add `api_key=` to the URL above to "fix" a 401 in production. A key in a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable is a key printed in every visitor's network log; the
# right fix is to authorize the domain.
#
# Local development needs nothing: Stadia serves localhost and 127.0.0.1 without
# a key, under strict rate limits. Sustained local work that starts returning
# HTTP 429 is the signal to get an account key for server-side use, not to paste
# one in here.
#
# ## Why these are NEXT_PUBLIC_
#
# A tile URL appears in every request the browser makes and the attribution is
# printed on the map by design, so neither is a secret and neither can be made
# one. Nothing that must stay private — see the SMTP block — may ever take the
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# ## The rules the resolver enforces
#
# Configure the URL and the attribution together or not at all. Setting one
# without the other, or a template missing a placeholder, is refused rather than
# patched over: the map is not drawn, a notice says so, and the list of places
# below it is unaffected. Serving one provider's tiles under another provider's
# copyright line is the one failure a licence actually punishes.
#
# Leave all three blank and the map falls back to the public OpenStreetMap tile
# endpoint. That fallback exists so a fresh checkout has a working map with no
# setup; it is an external community service run by the OpenStreetMap
# Foundation, not Armat infrastructure, and **production must set the three
# values below rather than rely on it**. No availability, throughput or coverage
# guarantee is made here about any provider, including Stadia.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_URL=https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION='&copy; <a href="https://stadiamaps.com/" target="_blank">Stadia Maps</a> &copy; <a href="https://openmaptiles.org/" target="_blank">OpenMapTiles</a> &copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright" target="_blank">OpenStreetMap</a>'
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_MAX_ZOOM=20
